1. System Monitoring:

 Command:
    top
 What it does:
    Shows running processes and system resource usage in real time.
 What I learned:
     I can use it to check CPU and memory usage and see which processes are using system resources.

 Command:
    free -h
 What it does:
    Shows information about RAM and swap memory.
 What I learned:
    It helps me check how much memory is being used and how much is available.

 Command:
    df -h
 What it does:
    Shows the used and available disk space on mounted filesystems.
 What I learned:

 It helps me check whether a filesystem is running out of disk space.

 Command:
    du -sh /var/ *
 What it does:
      Shows how much disk space files and directories are using.
 What I learned: 
      It can help identify which directories are taking up more disk space.

 Command:
    vmstat
 What it does:
    Shows information about processes, memory, CPU, and system activity.
 What I learned:
    It gives a quick overview of system resource usage.


2. Services :


 Command:
     systemctl status ssh
 What it does:
     Shows the current status of the SSH service.
 What I learned:
     I can use it to check whether a service is running, stopped, or has encountered a problem.

Command:
    systemctl is-enabled ssh
What it does:
    Checks whether the SSH service is configured to start automatically when the system boots.
What I learned:
    It helps me check whether a service is enabled to start at boot.

Command:
    sudo systemctl start <service>
What it does:
    Starts a service.
What I learned:
    I can use it when a required service is currently stopped.

Command:
    sudo systemctl stop <service>
What it does:
    Stops a running service.
What I learned:
    It can be used when a service needs to be stopped.

Command:
    sudo systemctl restart <service>
What it does:
    Stops and starts a service again.
What I learned:
    Restarting a service can be useful after making configuration changes or when troubleshooting a service.

Command:
     systemctl list-units --type=service
What it does:
     Lists currently loaded service units.
What I learned:
     It helps me see the services managed by systemd.

Command:
     journalctl -u ssh
What it does:
     Shows logs related to the SSH service.
What I learned:
     I can use service logs to investigate problems when a service is not working as expected.
